WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:05.590
Hello, everyone and
welcome to today's session

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:10.650
of the BD2K Fundamentals of
Data Science webinar series,

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.390
where each week we present
a new lecture giving us

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:17.680
a background in the
foundations of data science.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.390
And it's a real pleasure to
introduce today's speaker.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.210
Her name is Lucila Ohno-Machado.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.230
Lucila is Professor of Medicine
at the University of California

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.710
San Diego and Chair of the
Department of Biomedical

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:30.710
Informatics.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:31.780
Lucila has had a
distinguished career.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.559
She received her MD from
the University of San Paulo,

00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:39.070
received her MBA, went
to Stanford in the 1990s,

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.910
received her PhD.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.359
Musen, and I can
say that I'm pleased that she

00:00:43.359 --> 00:00:46.559
got her PhD with me, which
makes me very, very proud.

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:48.359
Lucila has lots of honors.

00:00:48.359 --> 00:00:52.269
She's an elected member of the
American Society for Clinical

00:00:52.269 --> 00:00:55.049
Investigation and a fellow
of the American College

00:00:55.049 --> 00:00:56.100
of Medical Informatics.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.139
She edits the Journal of the
American Medical Informatics

00:00:59.139 --> 00:01:00.139
Association.

00:01:00.139 --> 00:01:01.789
But most important,
she directs a project

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:05.400
through the BD2K program
related to finding, accessing,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.180
interoperating with
and using data.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.570
And that's going to be the topic
of Lucila's talk this morning.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.280
Before Lucila speaks,
let me remind everyone

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.241
that you're all on mute
because there's so many people

00:01:16.241 --> 00:01:18.800
listening into this call.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.730
As you have questions
during the talks today,

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.510
please put your questions
into the chat box and we'll

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.130
be sure that we can
send those questions

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.300
to Lucila for her comment
at the end of the hour.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.910
And we're looking forward to
hearing what Lucila has to say.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:35.910
Go ahead.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.230
Thank you, Mark, and
thanks to the organizers

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:41.480
for the opportunity to
present this project, which

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.200
is the Data Discovery Index
Consortium that we are leading

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.640
together with collaborators
from the University of Michigan,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:57.390
University of Oxford, and also
University of Texas Houston.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.979
So I'm presenting on
behalf of all of them.

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:06.470
And I'll try to monitor the
question box as I speak.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.560
So I would like to start
with the motivation for this.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.459
And this is one that I'm
personally interested in.

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:20.079
For example, a question about
could I get all data about

00:02:20.079 --> 00:02:23.499
astrocytomas with
TP53 mutations.

00:02:23.499 --> 00:02:29.829
And, in particular, I'm
interested in the stage two

00:02:29.829 --> 00:02:31.159
type of tumors.

00:02:31.159 --> 00:02:32.670
Can I get images?

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.140
Can I get medical records?

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:38.760
Can I get other type of data
so I could assess survival

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.090
or I could assess recurrence
in other items of interest?

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.450
And, believe it or not, if
you're trying to do that today,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.680
it is very cumbersome and
you don't find everything

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.120
that you should.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.680
So we started with the Data
Discovery Index Consortium

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.531
with the goal of helping
users find shared data,

00:02:58.531 --> 00:03:02.410
build a prototype for data
discovery index, a search

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:06.249
engine, and interoperate
in the Commons.

00:03:06.249 --> 00:03:10.389
So in accordance to
the FAIR principles,

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:14.950
our primary goals are
to find and access data.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.310
And the other I and
R are also desirable,

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:23.469
but are not the total focus
of our projects as of now.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.879
Our goal is to do
for data what PubMed

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:29.419
did for papers, that is
make them discoverable

00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:32.209
and make them accessible.

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:35.489
BioCADDIE started effectively
about 1 and 1/2 years

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:38.080
ago with our set of priorities.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.640
And I'm pleased to say
that after 1 year and 1/2

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.029
we have a lot to show.

00:03:45.029 --> 00:03:46.890
This is a slide
from Ron Margolis

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.769
that he presented at our
all hands meeting that

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:53.260
happened just a few weeks ago.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.449
And he had the task
to show others,

00:03:57.449 --> 00:03:59.921
or to show the
bioCADDIE team, where

00:03:59.921 --> 00:04:04.040
we fit into the digital
data Commons for NIH.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:08.349
And in his slide here,
he shows this stack

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:11.409
of items that are
anticipated for the Commons

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:14.779
and how data and
reference data sets are

00:04:14.779 --> 00:04:19.019
such an important foundation
for that, and with that,

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:24.850
all digital objects as well
with software, with systems

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.310
workflows, and so on.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.120
And this is all on top of
Compute Platform, which

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.120
can be cloud.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.070
It can be high performance
computing clusters.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:39.230
So in this slide I
just show my view

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:43.220
that pre-curated data
turns into curated data.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.320
And it's annotated as
metadata are included.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.510
And then analytics happen.

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:54.880
In analytics, it is the
priority of many of the BD2K

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:55.880
centers of excellence.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.730
And this is how it
all ties together.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:02.060
They are hosted in a
cloud, a cluster server.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.160
In our case, we do a
lot with HIPAA data,

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.780
not necessarily in bioCADDIE,
but in many other projects,

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.820
that is human
subjects that needs

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:16.440
to be protected with a
certain type of regulations.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.990
And it's often the case
that you combine this data.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:24.420
So in my example, you are
looking for medical records

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.230
determining the
rate of recurrence,

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:29.710
the survival of a
particular type of tumor,

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:34.170
so you want to combine
publicly available data

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:37.120
with HIPAA compliant data.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:42.360
And then when you join items,
you create another dataset.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.830
And that other dataset
can also be annotated

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:51.220
and be pre-processed and entered
into an analytical process.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:53.780
So that's why I believe
it all comes together

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:57.220
and we should join
forces in terms

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.500
of indexing all these
objects so we can reuse them

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:05.590
and can make the analysis
reproducible later.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:09.930
And journals and publications
are not out of the loop

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.470
because they are the end
product of all of this.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.190
And they're a little
bit of a circle,

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:19.250
in a loop in themselves.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.770
So the bioCADDIE project,
the main components

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:25.560
are working groups,
metadata specifications,

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.500
software development,
collaborations,

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:28.820
and administration.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:32.480
I actually proposed this
project initially thinking

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.650
it would be a research project.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:39.100
But clearly it was also an
engineering and technical

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.100
project.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.020
But most of all, it
is a social project

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:45.240
in which collaborations
are critical

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.770
and engagement of
a large community

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:51.870
makes the project
much more impactful.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:56.670
So the way we view bioCADDIE
and its Data Discovery Index

00:06:56.670 --> 00:07:00.870
prototype is that we will
develop a search engine

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:06.220
that people can access to show
what is involved in getting

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.740
digital objects indexed.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.360
This search engine,
as with anything

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.430
else, can be replaced by
another search engine,

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.320
another front end, and so on.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.660
But what's important is that
the data and the objects

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:23.550
get indexed in such a
way that this search

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:25.520
engine can find them.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.490
Additionally, we
want pilot projects

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:31.471
from other institutions to
be integrated into the Data

00:07:31.471 --> 00:07:33.430
Discovery Index so
people can develop

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.000
different portions of this DDI.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.730
And together we get much
further than we would otherwise.

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:45.190
An important aspect,
also, is that we

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:48.400
want to index data aggregators.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.970
That means we want to
index other indices.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.830
We don't want to
reproduce that work.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:58.500
And, in fact, we want to make
sure users can get to them

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.760
when they are searching
for particular objects.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:05.990
Like I said, in our case, we're
searching across a wide variety

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:07.170
of data types.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:11.680
And those aggregators tend to
focus on a particular data type

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:14.150
and are much more detailed
than we would ever

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:20.110
be able to be if we set out
to index absolutely everything

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:21.389
ourselves.

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:26.370
So one example that
will be given here

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:31.560
is the Omics Data Discovery,
the Data Index project,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.560
that I'll show.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:39.490
And it's in collaboration
with the Heart BD2K center

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.630
and with the ELIXIR colleagues.

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:46.060
So what we have here is just
another illustration of-- we

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:49.670
have big data sets, for example,
from the NIH conference that we

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:53.030
will index, we are
indexing at this moment.

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:57.220
We are also providing
links that are

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:59.930
indexed to aggregator services.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:06.890
So this was a pilot project
that Henning Hermjakob

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:11.460
leads in collaboration with
bioCADDIE and the Heart BD2K

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:12.920
center.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.470
And it's the Omics
Discovery Index.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:19.230
So, again, it has
its own search engine

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:25.010
and it has indexed Omics
objects to a very high extent.

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:28.750
So what we do is integrate
with this aggregator,

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:32.190
and, therefore, not
replicate the work.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:34.690
But let's say we were
trying my search.

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:39.550
We would find the Omics
datasets on astrocytomas,

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:42.390
but we would also find
images, hopefully,

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.590
and find clinical data.

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:47.390
So that's the goal
of being an umbrella

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:53.390
index for all those other
ones that we help integrate.

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:57.210
So the OmicsDI is one
of several examples.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:00.600
But it's the one that
was a supplement funded

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:04.740
to the project and I'm
happy to display here.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.470
There are others as well.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:13.080
So the goal is to get these
repositories, the datasets,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.850
and the date aggregators,
and adjust that metadata

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:21.140
so we can do this uber
index into a search engine,

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:26.390
provide users with pointers,
with results that are ranked

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:28.310
in order of relevance to them.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:31.260
In order to do that, we
have a terminology server

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:35.940
that attempts queries and
also a ranking engine.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:39.700
So some of you have
seen our presentation

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.630
at the last BD2K meeting.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:45.022
We were in the beginning of
the road or in the middle here.

00:10:45.022 --> 00:10:48.820
And we were designing
the prototype

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:53.120
so we can showcase what
the indexing is all about.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:57.640
Where we are now, this
data engine, DataMed,

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:59.610
is already deployed.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.360
And it's in version 1.0.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:02.630
That you can see.

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:12.080
And together with it, it's
the metadata, which is also

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:13.850
finalized at this point.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:17.871
So the front end of data
metalogs like this-- there

00:11:17.871 --> 00:11:23.510
are 23 repositories now, many
more being indexed as we speak,

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:26.990
10 data types, 4 pilot
projects integrated,

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:32.690
and about 649,000
datasets at the moment.

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:34.960
Also, at the bottom
here you will

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:39.000
see the pilot projects that
have already been integrated

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:40.550
with three more to come.

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:42.990
And the way the
pilot projects work

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:46.370
is we issue a request
for applications.

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:51.020
And we have a peer
review process and then

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:55.780
issue subcontracts
to the projects

00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.760
that were deemed most
relevant and most integrable

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:01.760
with bioCADDIE.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.870
And here we have a
DataMed search engine,

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:05.870
how it looks like.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.810
I encourage you all
to go to DataMed.org

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:12.279
and try it out for yourselves.

00:12:12.279 --> 00:12:16.110
There are filtering by
facets, by data type,

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.970
by repository, by accessibility,
and authorization.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:22.690
And so keep in
mind that in order

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:24.540
to filter by
accessibility, we do

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:28.070
need to have the
metadata about that.

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:30.700
And that's why one
of our working groups

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.650
has focused on it.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:36.250
We can, again, filter by
repositories, specific facets,

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:38.290
and so on.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:41.750
So upcoming, our path
ahead is depending

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.029
on a variety of factors.

00:12:43.029 --> 00:12:45.160
But the goal was to
make a difference

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.070
in the way the data are shared.

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:49.850
So we have a dataset
retrieval challenge.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:53.440
We are still having working
groups for certain items

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:58.560
that we are fine tuning and
integrating into the projects.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.740
So in terms of the working
groups that I talked about,

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:05.760
they are a fundamental
piece of bioCADDIE.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.980
They relate to use cases.

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:10.950
That's how we elicit
it-- use cases

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:15.070
through working groups define
criteria for inclusion,

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:17.300
define how we would
deal with identifiers,

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:18.440
define the metadata
specifications,

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:25.020
and what the graphical interface
would look like, usability

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:27.779
testing, and so on,
and, importantly, also

00:13:27.779 --> 00:13:32.330
data citations and links to
publications, and then finally,

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:33.830
the evaluation.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:36.090
So these were all
working groups that we

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:40.230
created surrounding the Data
Discovery Index Consortium so

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:44.450
that we could get members from
the community to participate

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:46.149
in the creation of it.

00:13:46.149 --> 00:13:48.911
So one of the initial
working groups

00:13:48.911 --> 00:13:51.930
is how we interacted with
the other BD2K centers

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:54.950
and other programs at NIH.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:59.060
So we had developed some
supplemental projects--

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:03.480
I will present one
of them quickly--

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:07.970
with other centers shown here.

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:10.680
And there is one upcoming
with links as well,

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.640
and visualization, and ranking.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:14.700
As I said, there's
often different modules

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:18.880
that can be either incorporated
to DataMed as a search engine

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:22.700
or be a search
engine in themselves.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:26.660
We also have been in
contact with Common Fund

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:30.890
program about datasets that
they would like to be indexed.

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:38.440
And we had joint meetings all
through the streaming webinar.

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:41.130
We also plan to participate
in the called pilots.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:45.250
With the identifiers, we decided
we will not mint identifiers

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:47.760
through bioCADDIE, but
we will use identifiers

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:51.770
that fully resolve to an URI.

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:55.190
And we should also maintain
a set of landing pages

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.610
for each of the datasets
indexed, including

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:00.690
forward ones that
may no longer exist,

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:04.970
so we needed to document the
persistence of this dataset.

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:10.700
The Identifier Working Group
was led by Jeff Grethe.

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:15.310
And there was a minimal
identifier pilot

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:18.920
in collaboration with
other BD2K centers

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:24.520
that is also ongoing, as well
as Data Citation Implementation

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:31.250
Pilot with the FORCE11
organization led by Tim Clark.

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:33.450
This Data Citation
Implementation Project

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:37.390
was a supplement to
bioCADDIE and allowed

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:39.200
even more members
of the community

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:43.899
to contribute to our efforts.

00:15:43.899 --> 00:15:46.640
Another working group was
led by Susanna Sansone-- very

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:50.420
important, related to the
metadata specifications

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:53.220
and has gone through
several versions.

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:54.450
Now it's on 2.1.

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:56.740
And the important
thing to emphasize,

00:15:56.740 --> 00:16:00.140
it was derived both from
a use case approach, which

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:04.260
we call a top-down approach
shown on the left-hand side,

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.490
as well as a bottom-up
approach, that

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:10.190
is looking into what
we had as metadata

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:15.600
already and trying to
reconcile several metadata

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:19.460
strategies in order to come up
with the minimal specifications

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:25.490
for DATS, which is the Data
Tag Suite, which is based

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:29.899
on the same principle as JATS,
which is the Journal Article

00:16:29.899 --> 00:16:31.029
Tag Suite.

00:16:31.029 --> 00:16:35.610
Meaning, at one point, once
that specification is very well

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:44.330
understood, data producers can
submit data or submit metadata

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:50.990
to bioCADDIE in the same way
that journal publishers submit

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:52.510
data to PubMed.

00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:53.920
So that's our goal.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:59.290
The working group representation
was from several institutions.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.690
And some of them
are listed here.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:05.269
And this is to say that
it wasn't us deciding

00:17:05.269 --> 00:17:10.220
on the metadata specification,
but consultation

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:13.230
with many, many initiatives.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:16.780
We also did use cases
and testing benchmarks.

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:21.560
And there is a report you can
find at how user analysis was

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:24.270
done and usability tests.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:28.740
We also, as part of this,
created testing benchmarks.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.960
So that's another
contribution that we perceive

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:34.150
as being long-lasting
because it's

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:40.010
a very laborious work in terms
of annotating and confirming

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:44.420
and validating
rankings of retrievals.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.010
So this is now available.

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:48.810
And it will be used
for our challenge.

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:54.040
And after that, it will be
available to the community.

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:57.420
So the use cases
were essentially

00:17:57.420 --> 00:18:01.520
creating these
benchmark data sets,

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:04.160
instantiating the queries,
running search engines,

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:08.890
and manually annotating
the quality of results.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:12.960
We also had a working group
on Data Citation Metrics

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:14.970
because they are
important in order

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.450
to reward those
who share the data.

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:23.090
And the goals were to have
qualitative and quantitative

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:30.090
metrics that would talk
about issues such as when

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:35.400
the date were produced,
provenance, downloads, and then

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:39.679
rank results, and criteria
to restrict search that

00:18:39.679 --> 00:18:42.679
could depend on search
metrics, for example,

00:18:42.679 --> 00:18:47.770
the most popular datasets, that
is the most downloaded and so

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:48.770
on.

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:50.720
So this group dealt
specifically with this issue.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:54.470
And the report is
also available online.

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:57.890
Another working group
that is very important

00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:01.290
was to decide which
data repositories

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:06.150
we would index to get started
with our search engine.

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:08.480
And that is an
important distinction

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:12.559
between a search engine that
simply finds everything.

00:19:12.559 --> 00:19:17.380
We wanted to convert at least
some degree of filtering

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:21.120
of quality control to
which datasets appear,

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:23.441
or at least in the
order in which they

00:19:23.441 --> 00:19:27.120
appear in our search engine.

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:31.280
So we have come up with
some criteria here related

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:35.910
to prominence, sustainability,
scope, quality, and access,

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:39.500
and also with a
repository submission

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:44.850
workflow so that repositories
can approach us or we approach

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:45.850
them.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:52.190
And then we work out on how we
should automate this metadata

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:54.910
mapping into our
specifications and how

00:19:54.910 --> 00:20:00.270
to exert quality control
into the mapping and so on.

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:04.120
So, again, anyone who has
a repository out there who

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:08.700
wants it to appear right away
in bioCADDIE and DataMed, please

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:13.270
do let us know because
we are into a very

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:15.600
active process of doing this.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:20.799
Many repositories are
being indexed as we speak.

00:20:20.799 --> 00:20:24.120
And accessibility metadata
was also very important.

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:28.170
So lead by George Alter from
the University of Michigan,

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:32.570
a working group
decided what would

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:37.550
be the initial set of metadata
related to authorization,

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:39.490
authentication, and access.

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:45.720
And this has also been
incorporated into DATS 2.1.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:50.420
And why is
accessibility important?

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:56.140
Because we do not want to limit
ourselves to public data only.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:59.600
We know that there are several
very valuable datasets that

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:01.630
are under access control.

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:04.610
And users should
know that they exist

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:08.310
and then know also the
conditions by which they

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:10.580
could be accessible.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:13.350
So privacy is very
important to us.

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:16.630
And one important project,
a supplemental project

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:19.640
that relates to it, is
named Count Everything.

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:23.080
And that is for secure
aggregating counts

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:27.300
of relevant patients across
different data types.

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:30.880
So we partnered with
the MD2K center,

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:33.710
with the PIC-SURE
center, and also

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:36.300
the Global Alliance for
Genomics and Health,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:40.800
which is the translational
genomic center out of UC Santa

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:45.340
Cruz in order to come up with
a secure aggregated account.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:48.090
Why is it important
for bioCADDIE?

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:50.260
The situation is as
follows-- remember,

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:53.430
I was looking for astrocytomas.

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:59.230
And suppose I was
looking for ones

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:07.440
that had mutations in TP 53
and I wanted the whole sequence

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:10.429
of that particular gene.

00:22:10.429 --> 00:22:14.760
If I go by metadata only,
I would know that, yes,

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:21.050
I have whole genome sequence
data for those patients

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:22.050
that I identify.

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:24.480
However, I would
not know how many

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:27.299
have a particular mutation.

00:22:27.299 --> 00:22:32.080
So if it's a dataset that has
no patients with mutations,

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.419
maybe it's not as
important to me

00:22:34.419 --> 00:22:39.030
as having another dataset
in which 20% of the patients

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:42.929
have mutations in that
particular gene of interest.

00:22:42.929 --> 00:22:47.220
So in order to organize the
way I would be requesting

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:51.510
those datasets, because they
are under access control,

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:53.130
it would be important
to know which

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:56.770
one has more of the
characteristics of the patients

00:22:56.770 --> 00:22:58.270
I'm looking for.

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:01.530
So that's why counting
everything would be important.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:05.900
But as you know, counting
can compromise privacy.

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:09.400
So that's where we
designed this in such a way

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:11.601
that it's done
through encryption.

00:23:11.601 --> 00:23:16.990
And all those communications
and the aggregation

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:19.190
is done in a secure environment.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:21.120
So this is a
supplemental project

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:25.220
that involves those three
other centers of excellence,

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:28.390
those other BD2K centers.

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:30.690
Continuing on the list
of working groups--

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:35.809
also a working group to
rank search results led

00:23:35.809 --> 00:23:39.309
from the University of Texas
in Houston was conducted.

00:23:39.309 --> 00:23:40.730
The report is online.

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:44.640
And their recommendations
are to empirically determine

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:47.610
which ranking algorithm from
the Elasticsearch family

00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:51.650
would be best for us based on
evaluations against reference

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:56.110
sets and logs of user behavior--
since we now have a user basis

00:23:56.110 --> 00:23:59.190
and they are continuously
accessing DataMed--

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:03.970
and provide alternative ranking
based on user preferences.

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:08.790
So this also is evolving as we
have more users to our search

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:10.490
engine.

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:14.289
End User Evaluation Criteria--
also very important.

00:24:14.289 --> 00:24:17.030
And we are still
conducting this in terms

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:19.020
of saying, how do we
know we got there,

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:23.700
that we have a good
product for users?

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:26.789
Usage itself might
not be a good metric.

00:24:26.789 --> 00:24:30.440
There can be other
measures of impact.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:35.370
So this particular working
group was working on that.

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:39.000
Repository Contact Workflow--
this was led by George Alter.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:44.840
How do we develop this
workflow in terms of having

00:24:44.840 --> 00:24:47.160
a repository to index?

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:49.620
Who starts the process?

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:51.840
How is it tracked?

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:53.510
And so on.

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:56.620
How we prioritize
those that come to us.

00:24:56.620 --> 00:25:02.210
There is now a committee
that has developed processes

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:06.100
and is following up with
this particular aspect.

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:09.360
Natural Language Processing
is also an important aspect

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:14.340
of our index
because, as you know,

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:18.870
it is very important to extract
structured data from narrative

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:19.870
text.

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:22.549
And, actually, we
have just issued

00:25:22.549 --> 00:25:28.320
an extension on our dataset
retrieval challenge sign up.

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:32.220
So that we came up as--
there's a challenge that

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:36.210
has a very high
monetary award which

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:39.169
will be in the form of a
subcontract to the winners.

00:25:39.169 --> 00:25:42.790
And there can be more than one.

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:48.419
Around $50,000 for this
particular challenge.

00:25:48.419 --> 00:25:52.070
So if you're interested, please
do log in to Biocaddie.org

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:55.270
and look into this challenge.

00:25:55.270 --> 00:25:59.250
The way it works
is that the winners

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:02.160
will have their
algorithms incorporated

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.710
into our data match prototype.

00:26:04.710 --> 00:26:11.929
And we will make full
use of their work.

00:26:11.929 --> 00:26:14.240
And we will compensate
for that as well.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:16.780
And an independent
panel of judges

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:19.980
will be making
that determination.

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:23.340
So, also, for
ingestion and indexing,

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:26.030
I won't go through
many, many details.

00:26:26.030 --> 00:26:28.419
I would ask you to
primarily follow

00:26:28.419 --> 00:26:34.220
the boxes that are shown
here in this L-shaped format.

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:39.090
Because that's the process
by which the core development

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:44.120
team, led by Jeff
Grethe and by Hua Xu,

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:47.549
are developing this
ingestion of data sources

00:26:47.549 --> 00:26:50.340
in order to get the metadata.

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:53.250
And there are important
interactions here,

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:58.669
and I would say that with
schema.org and with CEDAR.

00:26:58.669 --> 00:27:01.809
So with schema.org we want
to be perfectly aligned

00:27:01.809 --> 00:27:09.050
so that the metadata can
conform to schema.org

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:14.130
so it can be found by other
search engines and not

00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:15.580
only DataMed.

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:18.000
Also, the collaboration
with CEDAR

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:22.059
is such that-- remember, there
were datasets that were not

00:27:22.059 --> 00:27:27.679
already aggregated and that
the data contributors would

00:27:27.679 --> 00:27:33.840
be annotating those data sets
or creating those metadata,

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:35.780
populating the metadata for us.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:43.030
And we rely on CEDAR, that is
a center led by Mark Musen that

00:27:43.030 --> 00:27:46.730
is developing these
forms in smart forms

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:53.040
so that data producers can
annotate their datasets.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:56.890
Also, the DATS
metadata model was

00:27:56.890 --> 00:28:01.400
developed by a working group
led by Susanna from Oxford.

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:04.580
And we are creating this
metadata transformation

00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:07.500
so that it becomes
easier to reconcile

00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:10.900
all of those strategies.

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:14.170
The requirements
for the metadata,

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:17.480
as I mentioned
before, were driven

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.580
by multiple stakeholders.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:24.049
So as I said, it's
a social process

00:28:24.049 --> 00:28:28.690
of reconciling all of this and
making it so that we're all

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:30.850
swimming in the same direction.

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:34.041
There was collaboration across
these multiple working groups

00:28:34.041 --> 00:28:38.289
that I mentioned before in
order to get this ingestion

00:28:38.289 --> 00:28:43.090
and indexing going through a
process that initially-- like

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:46.559
in number one, there
is a submission format,

00:28:46.559 --> 00:28:50.059
we need to develop
adapters, and so on.

00:28:50.059 --> 00:28:56.929
Evolving to number three,
in which we hope, and we

00:28:56.929 --> 00:29:01.820
are structuring ourselves to
scale so that, similar to how

00:29:01.820 --> 00:29:05.650
journalists currently
provide data to PubMed,

00:29:05.650 --> 00:29:08.230
data providers will
provide data to DATS

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:11.870
without this more
manual process that

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:19.419
is currently involved in
populating the initial DataMed.

00:29:19.419 --> 00:29:23.100
So as we evolve,
we become happier,

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:32.200
and we also scale the index,
how encompassing it is.

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.390
We also want to
enhance metadata,

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:37.540
that this will be a
continuous process.

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:42.549
And it will continue to
involve the community in that

00:29:42.549 --> 00:29:47.429
as well as incorporate elements
of natural language processing

00:29:47.429 --> 00:29:49.210
as we progress.

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:55.830
The final document preparation--
again, that is a pipeline here.

00:29:55.830 --> 00:30:01.190
And in order to be scalable--
that pipeline process is

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:04.570
scalable horizontally--
large datasets

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:06.299
were full and has
been pipelined.

00:30:06.299 --> 00:30:11.720
And DATS 2.1 models are
in the works right now.

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:16.700
And there is a high volume
of operations dedicated

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:18.440
to this at the moment.

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:23.280
We want to populate the
index as much as possible.

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:25.340
Scalable index
services as well--

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:29.350
we rely heavily
on Elasticsearch.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:34.929
And we are also creating
APIs for developers

00:30:34.929 --> 00:30:38.100
so they can build
their own modules

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:42.940
and utilize the indexed
data in their own ways.

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:46.770
This is another pilot project
that I wanted to highlight.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.360
Pilot projects are,
like I said, those

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:53.370
that are competed through
a peer review process

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:56.780
and are awarded to
the ones that are

00:30:56.780 --> 00:31:00.169
deemed more relevant to
bioCADDIE at this moment.

00:31:00.169 --> 00:31:05.210
So one of them is led by Chris
Mungall from Lawrence Berkeley

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:08.630
National Laboratory
in collaboration

00:31:08.630 --> 00:31:13.779
with other programs
displayed here.

00:31:13.779 --> 00:31:19.559
And Chris' goal is to use
GitHub, YAML, and Markdown

00:31:19.559 --> 00:31:28.830
in order to make, as he puts it,
making metadata annotation fun.

00:31:28.830 --> 00:31:32.070
So it's a project
that, again, he

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:35.500
uses the capabilities
of GitHib in order

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:41.169
to have authors put
their metadata in there.

00:31:41.169 --> 00:31:43.220
And this is ongoing.

00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:46.720
But we've seen some
demonstrations of it already.

00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:50.630
And it is, again,
showing alternatives

00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:53.830
to what we had originally
thought the annotation

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:55.350
process could be.

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:58.559
And this is the value of
engaging the community.

00:31:58.559 --> 00:32:01.480
We always have some
good ideas coming up.

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:05.580
And we can incorporate them
or put different modules

00:32:05.580 --> 00:32:08.450
into our own development.

00:32:08.450 --> 00:32:12.799
Another bioCADDIE
pilot project was

00:32:12.799 --> 00:32:17.141
issued to the University of
Utah and to metadata data

00:32:17.141 --> 00:32:18.870
discovery and
integration to support

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:22.570
repurposing of
heterogeneous data

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:24.510
using the OpenFurther Platform.

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:28.260
And this is, again,
another way of indexing

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:32.870
that this particular group
in Utah has proposed.

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:37.160
And we said, yeah,
let's try that, too.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:39.039
Because the more
minds that think

00:32:39.039 --> 00:32:42.070
about this process, the better.

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:45.640
Another one to complete-- there
were three this past year--

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:49.730
was the feasibility study of
indexing clinical research data

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:56.860
using HL7 FHIR, which is a
standard for clinical data

00:32:56.860 --> 00:33:00.730
exchange that is very
popular these days.

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:05.029
And it's a pilot project
on a harvesting strategy

00:33:05.029 --> 00:33:10.940
for the data discovery index led
by Harold Solbrig from the Mayo

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:11.940
Clinic.

00:33:11.940 --> 00:33:13.360
So they had three deliverables.

00:33:13.360 --> 00:33:14.360
One was metadata harmonization.

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:20.561
The other one was indexing
the dbGap phenotype variables.

00:33:20.561 --> 00:33:24.140
And the third one
was exposing metadata

00:33:24.140 --> 00:33:26.840
through schema.org,
which as I have mentioned

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:32.409
before is also our goal
with that, with our metadata

00:33:32.409 --> 00:33:33.730
specification.

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:38.190
So we're very happy to
have those three pilot

00:33:38.190 --> 00:33:39.929
projects this year.

00:33:39.929 --> 00:33:44.980
And we'll have the natural
language processing next year.

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:47.799
And these are all,
again, available

00:33:47.799 --> 00:33:51.590
for the community to come
up with the best ideas.

00:33:51.590 --> 00:33:56.100
So we, essentially, see
bioCADDIE as having the Data

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:57.100
Shop component.

00:33:57.100 --> 00:34:00.140
This is very technical--
indexing link,

00:34:00.140 --> 00:34:02.850
linking to articles,
ontology-based tools.

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:07.890
We see the Data Showcase,
which is the front end that

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:10.270
uses Elasticsearch,
but essentially it's

00:34:10.270 --> 00:34:13.910
about the graphical
interface, how we visualize,

00:34:13.910 --> 00:34:19.600
how the usability of the
tool is meeting expectations.

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:21.120
And then there is the
whole Data Market,

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:24.409
which, again, it's a
social aspect of it

00:34:24.409 --> 00:34:25.690
that is very important.

00:34:25.690 --> 00:34:28.770
Because there need to
be incentives for data

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:35.530
sharing and for people to
annotate data very well.

00:34:35.530 --> 00:34:39.030
And those incentives currently
are somewhat obscure.

00:34:39.030 --> 00:34:43.429
And then, citation
networks need to develop,

00:34:43.429 --> 00:34:46.700
because the same way as
publication citations

00:34:46.700 --> 00:34:53.159
became the currency
of academic metrics,

00:34:53.159 --> 00:34:57.350
the data citations might
become in the future at least

00:34:57.350 --> 00:35:02.650
an important part of
the large ecosystem.

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:06.580
So in our bioCADDIE
Data Shop and Showcase,

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:11.960
we see as the repositories
contributing as something

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:13.621
happens inside this shop.

00:35:13.621 --> 00:35:15.610
And then the products
are displayed

00:35:15.610 --> 00:35:22.290
in an easy-to-see fashion
so that the researchers,

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:26.070
our clients or
customers, can decide

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:30.380
what they want to take
out of the store or not.

00:35:30.380 --> 00:35:33.730
As I said, the Data Market
needs to be developed.

00:35:33.730 --> 00:35:36.089
It is not fully developed.

00:35:36.089 --> 00:35:38.480
BioCADDIE is one
front end of it--

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:40.790
or DataMed is one
front end of it.

00:35:40.790 --> 00:35:41.920
There can be others.

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:46.359
And, again, the currency for
data has not been well-defined.

00:35:46.359 --> 00:35:51.210
So it's very exciting to
be at this point in time

00:35:51.210 --> 00:35:59.030
where we are helping define all
these elements for the NIH data

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:00.330
Commons.

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:05.420
And more than data we also see
these other digital objects

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:07.630
to have in this value.

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:09.400
And it will be software.

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:13.920
It will be software systems,
packaged as dockers,

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:20.830
packaged as VMs, or other sorts
of value that will be provided.

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:24.820
So we are, with the help
of a large community,

00:36:24.820 --> 00:36:28.839
trying to create the
market, define the market,

00:36:28.839 --> 00:36:35.350
and create the first tools,
so that after it exists,

00:36:35.350 --> 00:36:38.580
others can come up with
even better tools, and even

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:43.690
better ways of indexing,
of accessing, and so on.

00:36:43.690 --> 00:36:46.480
So we are at the beginning
of a long journey.

00:36:46.480 --> 00:36:50.170
So here is the roadmap
created by the University

00:36:50.170 --> 00:36:52.040
of Texas colleagues.

00:36:52.040 --> 00:36:57.099
And as you see, we have met
the milestones from before.

00:36:57.099 --> 00:36:58.940
We are still running there.

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:03.800
And there are public releases
of the new versions scheduled.

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:09.170
And they have improvements
on a variety of features

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:11.640
as determined by the community.

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:15.170
So please do experiment
with DataMed.

00:37:15.170 --> 00:37:17.540
Don't expect to see
everything in there.

00:37:17.540 --> 00:37:19.550
But it's there so
you get the idea

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:24.080
and you demand other datasets
and you demand improvements

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:29.950
in the user interface and
coverage of the datasets

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:31.920
that we have right now.

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:33.940
So our journey-- this
is a poor picture

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:36.900
that I took in the
Johannesburg airport.

00:37:36.900 --> 00:37:41.230
If you have an opportunity,
please do take a better one.

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:43.599
But it says something
very important.

00:37:43.599 --> 00:37:47.150
It says-- if you want to
go fast, you go alone.

00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:49.280
If you want to go far,
then you go together.

00:37:49.280 --> 00:37:51.220
It is an African proverb.

00:37:51.220 --> 00:37:56.380
And we see bioCADDIE
as in that stage

00:37:56.380 --> 00:38:01.200
that we were asked to go
fast, but we were also

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:03.080
asked to go far.

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:07.890
So we have engaged a large
community to go far with us,

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:12.710
to have an initial prototype
and then engaged the community

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:15.890
to challenge the community
to have better prototypes,

00:38:15.890 --> 00:38:20.200
and to help us index
this huge volume

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:22.340
of data that are
out there, unused,

00:38:22.340 --> 00:38:28.560
in a way that makes more
value for our dollars.

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:31.089
So with that, I
was also instructed

00:38:31.089 --> 00:38:34.470
to leave plenty of
time for discussion.

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:37.380
So I have here the
two things that I

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:40.349
want you to remember from
this talk-- Biocaddie.org

00:38:40.349 --> 00:38:45.650
and DataMed.org-- so that you
can fully understand what we're

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:49.580
doing through our
consultation with the website

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:52.550
and experimentation
with the search engine.

00:38:52.550 --> 00:38:57.160
So, again, I would like to thank
the organizers for having given

00:38:57.160 --> 00:38:59.610
me the opportunity to do this.

00:38:59.610 --> 00:39:04.980
I did not list here all
collaborators for this project

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:08.480
in fear that I
would miss somebody.

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:13.470
And with so many collaborating--
more than 100 at this point--

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:16.710
that would certainly happen.

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:19.600
So I would like to thank
all collaborators, all

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:23.190
our members of the executive
committee of bioCADDIE

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:31.450
who have teamed up to come up
with the best of both worlds.

00:39:31.450 --> 00:39:35.540
You know it's a consortium
that we put the best minds

00:39:35.540 --> 00:39:38.460
to work into this very,
very challenging problem.

00:39:38.460 --> 00:39:44.300
So, Mark, thank you,
again, for the opportunity.

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:45.760
Thanks, Lucila.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:47.950
That was great.

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:49.390
I'm encouraging all
of the people who

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:52.380
are dialed into this
webinar to post questions.

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:55.670
Since everyone is muted,
please put your questions

00:39:55.670 --> 00:39:59.980
into the chat box and we'll
try to get to them as we can.

00:39:59.980 --> 00:40:02.450
So please put your questions
in the chat box now.

00:40:02.450 --> 00:40:05.320
And we'll feed them to Lucila.

00:40:05.320 --> 00:40:08.829
Before we do that,
Lucila, let me ask you,

00:40:08.829 --> 00:40:11.780
you ended your talk
showing a roadmap.

00:40:11.780 --> 00:40:14.770
And bioCADDIE has a
very ambitious goal.

00:40:14.770 --> 00:40:17.119
How do you know
when you're done?

00:40:17.119 --> 00:40:20.860
Do you have any sense for what
is the number of repositories,

00:40:20.860 --> 00:40:23.510
what is the number of
datasets out there,

00:40:23.510 --> 00:40:26.470
and how can you ever know
when to stop working?

00:40:26.470 --> 00:40:30.470
Yeah, well, again, that's
why we had a full working

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:32.760
group on that, right?

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:38.480
But, also, yeah, I see we
have a lot to do still.

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:43.300
And I can say it has been
such a fast-paced project

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:46.240
that sometimes we don't
even have time to stop

00:40:46.240 --> 00:40:53.650
and think of what's next, how
do we know when we're done.

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:57.161
And we think at
this phase there is

00:40:57.161 --> 00:41:01.859
so much to be done that I
can't see the end very soon.

00:41:01.859 --> 00:41:05.079
So in contrast with
the publication

00:41:05.079 --> 00:41:09.460
world in which there
was a market already--

00:41:09.460 --> 00:41:11.130
journals existed,
they were publishing,

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:12.730
they were having
their own websites,

00:41:12.730 --> 00:41:18.609
and so on-- so in
contrast to publications,

00:41:18.609 --> 00:41:22.210
data are more complicated
because it's not as organized

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:24.950
as that market was.

00:41:24.950 --> 00:41:28.250
So at this point
we're just seeing

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:32.369
the tip of the iceberg, the
datasets that most people know

00:41:32.369 --> 00:41:36.330
and have used, except that
they're scattered all around,

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:41.390
and except that as a user of
a particular type of data,

00:41:41.390 --> 00:41:43.609
let's say for me
clinical data, I

00:41:43.609 --> 00:41:46.760
know where to tap
into the sources.

00:41:46.760 --> 00:41:49.540
But when I jump
into Image Data, I

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:51.970
don't know, or Omics, or others.

00:41:51.970 --> 00:41:56.290
So there is so much out
there that we don't even

00:41:56.290 --> 00:41:59.160
know-- that that's important
that those communities

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:03.370
specialized on these data
types be in this journey

00:42:03.370 --> 00:42:07.020
together with us so we
don't miss anything.

00:42:07.020 --> 00:42:10.310
But it is a very
important question,

00:42:10.310 --> 00:42:14.950
but one that, unfortunately,
I don't have a good answer

00:42:14.950 --> 00:42:15.950
for you right now.

00:42:15.950 --> 00:42:21.130
I just see so much
ahead of us that I can't

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:25.349
see the end at this point.

00:42:25.349 --> 00:42:28.829
Do you have any estimates
for what the denominator is?

00:42:28.829 --> 00:42:30.990
How much biomedical
data is out there

00:42:30.990 --> 00:42:32.720
and what fraction
can we possibly

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:34.160
access through bioCADDIE?

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:35.160
Yes.

00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:37.250
Well--
Is this is an

00:42:37.250 --> 00:42:38.280
impossible question?

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:39.280
No.

00:42:39.280 --> 00:42:42.150
I think the good
thing is at least,

00:42:42.150 --> 00:42:46.000
the repositories, we
know which ones they are,

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:47.630
what they are doing.

00:42:47.630 --> 00:42:52.190
And we also know
those repositories

00:42:52.190 --> 00:42:54.550
related to articles.

00:42:54.550 --> 00:42:59.130
Because those are increasingly
more prominent and more

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:00.130
available.

00:43:00.130 --> 00:43:02.589
What we don't know,
and actually what

00:43:02.589 --> 00:43:07.140
we don't know if
we really want, is

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:09.950
from the long tail of
those datasets that

00:43:09.950 --> 00:43:14.570
have not been indexed and are
potentially available but not

00:43:14.570 --> 00:43:18.950
findable, we don't know
the quality of all of that.

00:43:18.950 --> 00:43:23.480
So how do you know that
something is good enough

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:29.060
to appear in a search engine
that has quality control on top

00:43:29.060 --> 00:43:30.670
of it?

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:34.339
And you may say, well, maybe
it's not worth doing that

00:43:34.339 --> 00:43:39.829
and let the users exert
that quality control.

00:43:39.829 --> 00:43:42.960
As of today, and this
may change in the future,

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:46.810
but I believe that
it's important to get

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:51.460
started with some sense
of quality control first.

00:43:51.460 --> 00:43:56.430
And, therefore,
many datasets that

00:43:56.430 --> 00:44:01.040
has been produced by one
lab or kept there but not

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:03.730
shared, that there might be
some reason why they were not

00:44:03.730 --> 00:44:04.730
shared.

00:44:04.730 --> 00:44:08.190
So we will not being
addressing those immediately.

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:10.619
I think, as we
progress, and if we

00:44:10.619 --> 00:44:16.790
find that the denominator
is not that huge,

00:44:16.790 --> 00:44:21.460
then there is a case
for indexing everything.

00:44:21.460 --> 00:44:27.310
But probably what we'll find is
it will plateau at some point.

00:44:27.310 --> 00:44:30.369
But, again, I can't
tell at this point.

00:44:30.369 --> 00:44:35.760
I think it will take another
three or four years for us

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:40.670
to be at that stage that
we can really assess and be

00:44:40.670 --> 00:44:42.380
picky about this or that.

00:44:42.380 --> 00:44:43.380
OK.

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:46.710
We're starting to get some
questions from the audience.

00:44:46.710 --> 00:44:49.000
Probably the most important
comment that we've seen so far

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:50.910
comes from Marjorie
Mitchell who says,

00:44:50.910 --> 00:44:53.579
you're doing awesome work.

00:44:53.579 --> 00:44:55.849
Thank you.

00:44:55.849 --> 00:44:57.160
I read that.

00:44:57.160 --> 00:44:58.470
Thank you.

00:44:58.470 --> 00:44:59.470
OK.

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:02.950
I have a question asking
whether imaging data can

00:45:02.950 --> 00:45:06.960
be indexed with bioCADDIE, can
it be linked with Omics data.

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:10.910
And, ultimately, will bioCADDIE
give you one-stop shopping

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:12.180
so that you can have
a single search that

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:17.450
will provide results from a
variety of these kind of data

00:45:17.450 --> 00:45:18.450
sources?

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:19.450
That is my goal.

00:45:19.450 --> 00:45:22.660
And when I mentioned
that very first use case,

00:45:22.660 --> 00:45:27.190
I want to have all data about
this particular condition

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:30.070
because I want to correlate it.

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:33.770
I want to find
recurrence of a tumor

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:41.089
through images or through data
that shows increases in sizes

00:45:41.089 --> 00:45:42.950
and so on.

00:45:42.950 --> 00:45:48.120
So that's exactly the point
of getting an uber index that

00:45:48.120 --> 00:45:50.200
would then point
to the sources that

00:45:50.200 --> 00:45:51.960
have more detailed information.

00:45:51.960 --> 00:45:55.730
And if that's Omics, there
are a certain repositories.

00:45:55.730 --> 00:46:00.670
If that's imaging, there is
also, through the BD2K centers,

00:46:00.670 --> 00:46:04.970
excellent sources of
indexing of images.

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:09.320
So I think that our point
is exactly this integration.

00:46:09.320 --> 00:46:15.300
And you find things that
you didn't know about.

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:18.840
Another questioner
mentions that you

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:21.589
alluded to aggregating
patient data and points

00:46:21.589 --> 00:46:23.960
to the problem of there
not being unique patient

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:28.450
identifiers that make it easy
to bring patient data together

00:46:28.450 --> 00:46:32.320
and questions, how do you
deal with that problem?

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:33.710
How do you deal
with missing data?

00:46:33.710 --> 00:46:35.520
How do you deal with
double counting data?

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:40.760
What are the challenges in
doing this kind of aggregation?

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:42.440
These challenges are multiple.

00:46:42.440 --> 00:46:47.190
I mean, they not only come
from the privacy end of things,

00:46:47.190 --> 00:46:50.480
but exactly what you
mentioned, the duplication

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:54.190
of patients across
different datasets

00:46:54.190 --> 00:46:58.460
is a huge problem today
for research studies.

00:46:58.460 --> 00:47:03.780
So bioCADDIE will not-- again,
I will be very happy when

00:47:03.780 --> 00:47:07.010
we are at that point,
but right now, it's

00:47:07.010 --> 00:47:10.599
more at the point of
that, oh, this exists.

00:47:10.599 --> 00:47:12.869
And this Count
Everything project

00:47:12.869 --> 00:47:18.000
was the first attempt to see
how we would link-- because one

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:23.790
dataset has mutations, another
dataset has sensor data,

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:25.620
another dataset
has clinical data.

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:28.329
Assuming we have
the same patient

00:47:28.329 --> 00:47:30.461
represented in all
those three, how

00:47:30.461 --> 00:47:34.690
we would do a secure
linkage and also

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:37.800
privacy preserved in
secure linkage of records

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:39.960
so we could do this
de-duplication.

00:47:39.960 --> 00:47:43.300
So that's a very
important research area

00:47:43.300 --> 00:47:45.250
that I encourage
more people to get

00:47:45.250 --> 00:47:49.069
involved because it's
really going to be

00:47:49.069 --> 00:47:53.359
a bottleneck in the future.

00:47:53.359 --> 00:47:55.490
OK.

00:47:55.490 --> 00:47:58.359
A corollary, that is how is
the identification managed

00:47:58.359 --> 00:47:59.359
by bioCADDIE?

00:47:59.359 --> 00:48:03.730
Again, we do not delve
into the datasets.

00:48:03.730 --> 00:48:07.220
Therefore, the de-identification
is not an issue

00:48:07.220 --> 00:48:11.700
because we're mostly dealing
at the metadata level.

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:14.869
So if you want all
African-American patients

00:48:14.869 --> 00:48:22.770
with TP 53 mutation of a
certain type, initially,

00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:25.500
what we can point
is to datasets that

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:29.780
have that type of metadata that
would have race and ethnicity

00:48:29.780 --> 00:48:35.240
and would have mutations or it
would have gene information.

00:48:35.240 --> 00:48:37.780
But unless we delve
into the dataset,

00:48:37.780 --> 00:48:41.510
we can't count the number.

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:44.790
So at this point,
re-identification

00:48:44.790 --> 00:48:49.790
through our index
is not an issue.

00:48:49.790 --> 00:48:51.450
Lots more questions
coming in, Lucila.

00:48:51.450 --> 00:48:52.870
This one is interesting.

00:48:52.870 --> 00:48:57.160
Do you have any tips for
searching with DataMed?

00:48:57.160 --> 00:48:59.620
People have intuition about
how to optimize their Google

00:48:59.620 --> 00:49:03.369
searches, but are there
any good heuristics or ways

00:49:03.369 --> 00:49:09.069
in which users can optimize
their efficiency in exploring

00:49:09.069 --> 00:49:11.260
data searches which is something
that is not necessarily

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:12.650
intuitive to most people?

00:49:12.650 --> 00:49:16.240
Yeah, well, what I'll do is
I will go to Advanced Search

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:18.230
because that might
not be immediately

00:49:18.230 --> 00:49:20.079
obvious in the interface.

00:49:20.079 --> 00:49:24.140
But there is--
just as in PubMed--

00:49:24.140 --> 00:49:30.720
a way not to just go in and
type what your search is,

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:33.000
but to structure it better.

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:38.880
I'm trying to get to the slide
that has the interface here.

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:41.570
Yeah, here.

00:49:41.570 --> 00:49:44.850
So see there is on the
right-hand side an Advanced

00:49:44.850 --> 00:49:45.850
Search.

00:49:45.850 --> 00:49:48.330
So I think that's the
best way to go about it.

00:49:48.330 --> 00:49:50.160
And then as you
get your answers,

00:49:50.160 --> 00:49:53.470
you can filter by
the repositories,

00:49:53.470 --> 00:49:55.730
by the data types, on
this left-hand side.

00:49:55.730 --> 00:49:57.770
And you can also
check the synonyms

00:49:57.770 --> 00:50:00.349
that it's getting for you.

00:50:00.349 --> 00:50:03.790
Because the engine, once you
type something at the top,

00:50:03.790 --> 00:50:08.180
it uses that terminology
search to go about synonyms

00:50:08.180 --> 00:50:13.520
and expand hierarchically
that concept.

00:50:13.520 --> 00:50:17.010
So I think that those would
be ways to get started.

00:50:17.010 --> 00:50:20.210
And also, feedback,
let me put it here.

00:50:20.210 --> 00:50:22.040
If you have feedback
about something

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:25.700
we should be filtering
by and we're not doing,

00:50:25.700 --> 00:50:28.920
that would be very important
for us to know as well.

00:50:28.920 --> 00:50:31.079
Please do contribute
your comments.

00:50:31.079 --> 00:50:35.160
Along those lines,
a questioner asks,

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:37.369
how are data errors and
inconsistencies found, flagged,

00:50:37.369 --> 00:50:38.369
and corrected?

00:50:38.369 --> 00:50:39.570
Do you actually have a
mechanism for giving feedback

00:50:39.570 --> 00:50:43.750
to the repository managers?

00:50:43.750 --> 00:50:44.900
Yes.

00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:47.710
So I would use this GitHub.

00:50:47.710 --> 00:50:53.700
You click here and
you put your issue

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:55.760
there so that someone
can respond to you.

00:50:55.760 --> 00:50:58.579
And if those
corrections are needed,

00:50:58.579 --> 00:51:00.520
that's an excellent
way to provide us

00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:01.839
with that information.

00:51:01.839 --> 00:51:02.839
OK.

00:51:02.839 --> 00:51:05.170
Two related questions,
one of which

00:51:05.170 --> 00:51:07.000
is, how can someone
get a list of all

00:51:07.000 --> 00:51:09.140
the collaborators in bioCADDIE.

00:51:09.140 --> 00:51:12.880
And are there librarians
working on the project?

00:51:12.880 --> 00:51:15.150
Yes, there are librarians.

00:51:15.150 --> 00:51:21.650
The list, if you go
Biocaddie.org-- unfortunately,

00:51:21.650 --> 00:51:24.430
we need to work
on the interface.

00:51:24.430 --> 00:51:26.940
If you go to working
groups, you will

00:51:26.940 --> 00:51:33.069
see everyone listed under
a working group there.

00:51:33.069 --> 00:51:37.330
So I would say the list is
scattered into the working

00:51:37.330 --> 00:51:38.330
groups.

00:51:38.330 --> 00:51:39.490
There isn't a consolidated list.

00:51:39.490 --> 00:51:41.319
But if that's desirable,
that's something

00:51:41.319 --> 00:51:44.420
that we should probably do.

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:49.240
Because then I can put a link
on my acknowledgments lines

00:51:49.240 --> 00:51:50.760
to that particular list.

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:52.359
And that will cover everyone.

00:51:52.359 --> 00:51:53.910
Great idea.

00:51:53.910 --> 00:51:55.450
Thank you.

00:51:55.450 --> 00:52:00.970
Lucila, you stated that you
had certain expectations

00:52:00.970 --> 00:52:04.030
about searching data
when you created DataMed.

00:52:04.030 --> 00:52:05.840
And the questioner
wants to know,

00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:09.060
did anything surprise
you in your findings

00:52:09.060 --> 00:52:10.790
as you created the tool?

00:52:10.790 --> 00:52:17.099
Yeah, well, it is
still surprising me

00:52:17.099 --> 00:52:20.480
because if I were
to say I'm satisfied

00:52:20.480 --> 00:52:23.700
and I can do that
query in the beginning,

00:52:23.700 --> 00:52:26.109
that was something I
really wanted to do,

00:52:26.109 --> 00:52:28.520
was as a friend of
the family, I wanted

00:52:28.520 --> 00:52:33.620
to know what is the survival
for a particular age

00:52:33.620 --> 00:52:42.089
group, a young person, and I
think I'm not satisfied yet.

00:52:42.089 --> 00:52:44.490
You know, we can find some
certain things there right now,

00:52:44.490 --> 00:52:47.580
but we don't have enough
imaging repositories,

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:52.330
and I know that
those are coming,

00:52:52.330 --> 00:52:54.250
so it's not like
we're not able to,

00:52:54.250 --> 00:52:56.819
but it's not populated
to the extent

00:52:56.819 --> 00:52:59.240
that I would like at this point.

00:52:59.240 --> 00:53:01.400
I mean, it is much
better than if I

00:53:01.400 --> 00:53:06.490
go on Google and search for it
because it's essentially very

00:53:06.490 --> 00:53:09.690
hard to find
anything that is more

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:14.770
data-centric in the current
Google search engine.

00:53:14.770 --> 00:53:21.880
But still, again, my surprise
is that it's not as simple.

00:53:21.880 --> 00:53:26.060
I started this project with
a much more naive approach.

00:53:26.060 --> 00:53:29.819
This is a lot of
work, but it's simple.

00:53:29.819 --> 00:53:34.450
And now, it's a lot of
work and it's not simple.

00:53:34.450 --> 00:53:38.190
That's for sure.

00:53:38.190 --> 00:53:40.390
Another question-- how are
you encouraging researchers

00:53:40.390 --> 00:53:42.520
to deposit their data
into repositories?

00:53:42.520 --> 00:53:45.430
Isn't this just a lot
more work for them?

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:49.619
And what is bioCADDIE doing
to get access to more data?

00:53:49.619 --> 00:53:53.421
Yeah, so, again, that's
where the data citation

00:53:53.421 --> 00:53:57.420
groups are working on it and
also what the incentives are.

00:53:57.420 --> 00:54:02.510
I think we do need help with
that because remember when

00:54:02.510 --> 00:54:07.569
it happened that journals
started requiring deposit

00:54:07.569 --> 00:54:11.290
of data in order to
publish an article,

00:54:11.290 --> 00:54:12.819
people started doing it.

00:54:12.819 --> 00:54:15.940
Again, not perfect
metadata, but at least data

00:54:15.940 --> 00:54:19.180
were deposited and so on.

00:54:19.180 --> 00:54:23.790
It so happened that there were
repositories ready for that.

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:27.160
What happens now is
there are certain data

00:54:27.160 --> 00:54:31.640
types in certain modalities that
you don't have repositories.

00:54:31.640 --> 00:54:37.910
Or, I mean, you may have, but
they're not as recognizable

00:54:37.910 --> 00:54:41.569
as those were when
the journalists made

00:54:41.569 --> 00:54:43.180
that requirement.

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:47.849
So I think we need to create,
again, this market for data

00:54:47.849 --> 00:54:52.280
repositories that are
beyond what exists already

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:55.230
for other data types.

00:54:55.230 --> 00:54:57.690
And then with the
publications, which

00:54:57.690 --> 00:55:01.170
are still important for
the academic community,

00:55:01.170 --> 00:55:02.170
require that.

00:55:02.170 --> 00:55:06.480
And then have the funding
agencies provide incentives

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:11.150
for people to comply with that.

00:55:11.150 --> 00:55:13.780
A questioner wants
to know how users

00:55:13.780 --> 00:55:16.059
might be engaged in
providing feedback

00:55:16.059 --> 00:55:17.430
to the DataMed developers.

00:55:17.430 --> 00:55:22.390
And how do you ensure that
you're creating a search engine

00:55:22.390 --> 00:55:24.950
that people actually
want versus one that you

00:55:24.950 --> 00:55:26.910
think they might want?

00:55:26.910 --> 00:55:27.910
Yes, exactly.

00:55:27.910 --> 00:55:30.050
And that's where,
again, I'll get

00:55:30.050 --> 00:55:34.089
to this point of providing
the feedback through GitHub

00:55:34.089 --> 00:55:40.520
or through contacting any of
us because each person has

00:55:40.520 --> 00:55:43.900
a different idea of
what they would want

00:55:43.900 --> 00:55:46.760
from a search engine on data.

00:55:46.760 --> 00:55:51.329
So that's also the reason
we had those pilot project

00:55:51.329 --> 00:55:54.920
solicitations and so on
because it is different

00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:56.400
and everyone is different.

00:55:56.400 --> 00:56:03.150
We want to know how can
we cover the 80% most

00:56:03.150 --> 00:56:06.980
common expectations
and then also learn

00:56:06.980 --> 00:56:11.030
about others so that in the
future we can produce those.

00:56:11.030 --> 00:56:18.000
So the best thing is to provide
feedback through this GitHub

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:23.710
link, which is here.

00:56:23.710 --> 00:56:25.190
So noted.

00:56:25.190 --> 00:56:26.359
We have very little time left.

00:56:26.359 --> 00:56:29.900
Let me be very selective in
posing some questions to you,

00:56:29.900 --> 00:56:30.900
Lucila.

00:56:30.900 --> 00:56:33.510
Here's one that says,
a dataset actually

00:56:33.510 --> 00:56:37.870
gets used and re-used-- or
rather, as a dataset actually

00:56:37.870 --> 00:56:40.220
gets used and re-used,
and suitability

00:56:40.220 --> 00:56:41.820
for different purposes
can become clearer,

00:56:41.820 --> 00:56:45.050
is there now in your
scheme, or do you

00:56:45.050 --> 00:56:47.200
envision developing,
rules for data users

00:56:47.200 --> 00:56:49.490
to supplement data
supplier's metadata

00:56:49.490 --> 00:56:53.400
with usage notes, caveats,
bug reports, et cetera.

00:56:53.400 --> 00:56:58.079
In other words, I guess, how
do you make DataMed two-way?

00:56:58.079 --> 00:56:59.079
Yes.

00:56:59.079 --> 00:57:00.579
So that's one thing
that we were thinking,

00:57:00.579 --> 00:57:05.990
too, that with more volume
and if we could-- for example,

00:57:05.990 --> 00:57:09.530
we are looking at it's screen
right now-- if we could have

00:57:09.530 --> 00:57:12.960
for each one of those
entries, the number

00:57:12.960 --> 00:57:17.069
of stars, the reviews
from people-- I liked it

00:57:17.069 --> 00:57:19.270
or no I didn't like
it, similar to when

00:57:19.270 --> 00:57:22.869
you were buying a
product on Amazon-- I

00:57:22.869 --> 00:57:25.079
think that would be very good.

00:57:25.079 --> 00:57:28.630
We do need to create that
feedback mechanism in here.

00:57:28.630 --> 00:57:34.980
And then we need to, obviously,
start getting a lot of usage

00:57:34.980 --> 00:57:39.220
to it so that we go
from several reviews.

00:57:39.220 --> 00:57:43.599
But I think that's
a goal definitely.

00:57:43.599 --> 00:57:51.059
A good question from
a user is, can you

00:57:51.059 --> 00:57:53.160
say something more about
how bioCADDIE actually

00:57:53.160 --> 00:57:54.319
facilities data re-use?

00:57:54.319 --> 00:58:00.829
Yeah, at this point I
wouldn't go that far.

00:58:00.829 --> 00:58:05.980
I would say we're trying to
address the findability of it.

00:58:05.980 --> 00:58:08.110
So indirectly if
you find it, you're

00:58:08.110 --> 00:58:13.500
more likely to re-use it,
but that's not a guarantee.

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:16.490
It might have access
controls that you are not

00:58:16.490 --> 00:58:20.800
able to fulfill the
requirements and so on.

00:58:20.800 --> 00:58:24.640
So I think it's a step
towards reusability,

00:58:24.640 --> 00:58:27.280
but it's run of
several components that

00:58:27.280 --> 00:58:30.630
need to be in place so
that it can be re-used.

00:58:30.630 --> 00:58:33.220
And this feedback,
again, is very important

00:58:33.220 --> 00:58:34.900
because people
have to access it.

00:58:34.900 --> 00:58:37.569
People have to use it and
then provide feedback.

00:58:37.569 --> 00:58:40.319
Yes, this was useful for me.

00:58:40.319 --> 00:58:45.730
Or, no, this dataset
looked good on the outside,

00:58:45.730 --> 00:58:48.829
but once I got it, it's
really not that useful.

00:58:48.829 --> 00:58:53.070
We're just about at
the end of the hour.

00:58:53.070 --> 00:58:54.370
Several questioners would
like to know how they

00:58:54.370 --> 00:58:56.460
can get ahold of your slides.

00:58:56.460 --> 00:59:00.010
I will provide it
to the organizers.

00:59:00.010 --> 00:59:02.160
And also, let me
make a point, I'll

00:59:02.160 --> 00:59:05.410
put it on the
Biocaddie.org website.

00:59:05.410 --> 00:59:07.590
Thanks, Lucila.

00:59:07.590 --> 00:59:11.829
And let me let the audience
know that this talk was recorded

00:59:11.829 --> 00:59:14.320
and will eventually be on
the website for the training

00:59:14.320 --> 00:59:17.950
coordinating center, which is
providing an archive of all

00:59:17.950 --> 00:59:21.480
of the webinars in the series.

00:59:21.480 --> 00:59:25.080
At this point, we
are out of time.

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:27.220
Let me thank Lucila
Ohno-Machado for what was

00:59:27.220 --> 00:59:28.710
a really exciting presentation.

00:59:28.710 --> 00:59:32.890
Let me thank all of you for
some really great questions

00:59:32.890 --> 00:59:35.080
and remind you that
we do this every week.

00:59:35.080 --> 00:59:37.920
And we look forward to next
week's presentation as well.

00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:39.300
Thanks, everyone.

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:40.680
Thank you.

